{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "the social network is a masterpiece of modern cinema that captures the chaotic birth of facebook with sharp precision. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film transforms a story about coding and college dorm rooms into a gripping shakespearean tragedy. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a likable hero but as a brilliant, socially awkward, and deeply ambitious figure who uses technology to bridge connections while remaining emotionally isolated himself. the screenplay is fast-paced and dense, filled with rapid-fire dialogue that keeps the audience on edge from the opening scene to the final shot. trent reznor and atticus ross composed an electronic score that feels cold and haunting, perfectly mirroring the sterile yet revolutionary world being created on screen. the movie explores themes of friendship, betrayal, class, and the elusive nature of success, asking whether creating a global community requires sacrificing one's own humanity. visually, the film is sleek and dark, using lighting and composition to emphasize the isolation of its characters despite their digital connectivity. it remains a definitive portrait of the internet age, showing how a simple idea can change the world while destroying the relationships of the people who built it. even years after its release, the social network stands as a timeless examination of ambition and the cost of innovation.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.13125396}, {"token": " social", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.11094197}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " masterpiece", "logprob": -2.519352}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0019291161}, {"token": " modern", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " cinema", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": " that", "logprob